# Credentials. The bundle you upload contains .env.production; the repository
# must never carry a database password, so it is excluded here and has to exist
# on the server already.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
# Server-owned, NOT application code. cPanel writes two delimited blocks into
# .htaccess on the server — the Passenger configuration and the environment
# variables from the Node.js App page — and it rewrites them whenever the app is
# saved or restarted.
#
# This repository is cloned directly INTO the app root, so tracking the same file
# gave it two writers and `git pull` started refusing to merge:
#
#   error: Your local changes to the following files would be overwritten by merge:
#           .htaccess
#
# Discarding those "local changes" would have thrown away cPanel's blocks, taking
# the Passenger interpreter path and the database credentials with them. The file
# is therefore left to cPanel, and .htaccess.example carries our own rules for
# whoever sets a server up next.
.htaccess

# Runtime data, written on the server. Next's file tracer copies the local
# equivalents of these into the build output on its own, so they are excluded
# here as well as emptied by the packaging script — a database backup archive
# must never reach this repository.
uploads/*
!uploads/.gitkeep
tmp/*
!tmp/.gitkeep
backups/*
!backups/.gitkeep

# Logs
*.log
npm-debug.log*
